1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,697
Here are three facts about a triangle: side a is twenty, side b is thirty, and angle A is thirty degrees.

2
00:00:07,697 --> 00:00:08,517
Draw it.

3
00:00:08,517 --> 00:00:09,093
Here it is.

4
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:10,526
And here it is again.

5
00:00:10,526 --> 00:00:16,580
A second triangle, completely different, that fits the very same three facts.

6
00:00:16,580 --> 00:00:17,687
Both are correct.

7
00:00:17,687 --> 00:00:26,320
So when a question gives you two sides and an angle that is not between them, the answer may be two triangles, or one, or none.

8
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:31,415
This unit shows you how to tell, and how to solve any triangle from any three parts.

9
00:00:36,900 --> 00:00:41,936
Welcome to Class 12 Mathematics, Unit 7: Solution of Triangles.

10
00:00:44,333 --> 00:00:45,572
Three parts.

11
00:00:45,572 --> 00:00:49,296
The four kinds of data and the law that matches each.

12
00:00:49,296 --> 00:00:54,191
The ambiguous case, with two sides and a non included angle.

13
00:00:54,191 --> 00:00:58,270
And heights and distances: towers, rivers and fields.

14
00:01:02,633 --> 00:01:09,666
To solve a triangle is to find all six parts, three sides and three angles, from the three you are given.

15
00:01:09,666 --> 00:01:11,971
There are only four patterns of data.

16
00:01:11,971 --> 00:01:13,325
Three sides.

17
00:01:13,325 --> 00:01:15,968
Two sides and the angle between them.

18
00:01:15,968 --> 00:01:18,116
One side and two angles.

19
00:01:18,116 --> 00:01:21,437
And two sides with an angle that is not between them.

20
00:01:21,437 --> 00:01:25,056
The first three always give exactly one triangle.

21
00:01:25,056 --> 00:01:27,042
The last is the troublemaker.

22
00:01:27,267 --> 00:01:32,322
And three angles alone will never do: they fix the shape but not the size.

23
00:01:33,533 --> 00:01:34,384
Three sides.

24
00:01:34,767 --> 00:01:39,193
The cosine law for A: thirty eight degrees thirteen minutes.

25
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:43,976
Again for B: cos B is exactly a half, so B is sixty.

26
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:47,704
Subtract for C: eighty one forty seven.

27
00:01:47,704 --> 00:01:51,571
The largest angle faces the largest side, eight.

28
00:01:51,571 --> 00:01:51,846
Good.

29
00:01:52,067 --> 00:02:00,219
And the area by Heron: s is ten, so the area is ten root three, seventeen point three two square centimetres.

30
00:02:02,233 --> 00:02:04,425
Two sides and the angle between them.

31
00:02:04,633 --> 00:02:08,122
The cosine law gives the third side at once: root seven.

32
00:02:08,333 --> 00:02:17,883
For the angles, Napier's tangent law gives both together: half the sum is sixty, half the difference nineteen degrees six minutes.

33
00:02:18,100 --> 00:02:23,149
Add and subtract: A seventy nine six, C forty fifty four.

34
00:02:25,167 --> 00:02:26,777
One side and two angles.

35
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,449
The third angle first: forty five.

36
00:02:29,667 --> 00:02:35,059
Then the sine law twice: twelve point two five and thirteen point six six.

37
00:02:35,267 --> 00:02:39,741
Check: the sides increase in the same order as their opposite angles.

38
00:02:45,333 --> 00:02:58,173
Given a, b and A. Draw angle A and side b, which fixes the point C. Side a must reach from C to the base line, so swing a compass of radius a about C.

39
00:02:58,173 --> 00:03:05,226
The shortest distance from C to the base is the altitude h, which is b sin A: fifteen here.

40
00:03:05,433 --> 00:03:10,109
If a is ten, shorter than h, the arc never reaches the base.

41
00:03:10,109 --> 00:03:10,868
No triangle.

42
00:03:11,100 --> 00:03:16,679
At a equals fifteen, exactly h, the arc just touches: one triangle, right angled.

43
00:03:16,900 --> 00:03:25,248
Between h and b, say twenty, the arc cuts the base twice, both in front of A: two triangles.

44
00:03:25,248 --> 00:03:26,903
This is the ambiguous case.

45
00:03:27,133 --> 00:03:33,085
And once a is at least b, the second crossing falls behind A, which is not allowed.

46
00:03:33,085 --> 00:03:34,312
Only one triangle again.

47
00:03:35,533 --> 00:03:41,719
So the test: compute h equals b sin A. Less than h: none.

48
00:03:41,719 --> 00:03:44,584
Equal to h: one, right angled.

49
00:03:44,584 --> 00:03:47,344
Between h and b: two.

50
00:03:47,344 --> 00:03:49,089
At least b: one.

51
00:03:49,089 --> 00:03:55,481
And if the given angle is obtuse, there is one triangle when a is longer than b, and none otherwise.

52
00:03:56,700 --> 00:03:58,088
The cold open's triangle.

53
00:03:58,300 --> 00:04:04,081
h is fifteen, and twenty lies between fifteen and thirty: two triangles.

54
00:04:04,300 --> 00:04:07,544
The sine law gives sin B equals point seven five.

55
00:04:07,544 --> 00:04:14,250
B is forty eight degrees thirty five minutes, or its supplement, one thirty one twenty five.

56
00:04:14,250 --> 00:04:17,422
Both work, since A plus B stays below one eighty.

57
00:04:17,633 --> 00:04:24,439
Solve each: c is thirty nine point two one in the first triangle, and twelve point seven five in the second.

58
00:04:26,467 --> 00:04:26,970
Two more.

59
00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:31,321
With a equal to ten, sin B would be one point five.

60
00:04:31,321 --> 00:04:32,923
No angle has that sine.

61
00:04:32,923 --> 00:04:33,677
No triangle.

62
00:04:33,900 --> 00:04:41,141
With a equal to forty, longer than b, only one triangle: B is twenty two degrees one minute.

63
00:04:41,141 --> 00:04:44,283
The obtuse option would push A plus B past one eighty.

64
00:04:44,500 --> 00:04:48,919
Then C and c follow: sixty three point zero six.

65
00:04:50,933 --> 00:04:54,880
The mark-losing habit: taking only the calculator's angle.

66
00:04:54,880 --> 00:05:00,237
Always write both B and one eighty minus B, then test each one.

67
00:05:02,667 --> 00:05:03,880
Pause and think.

68
00:05:03,880 --> 00:05:06,385
Compare a with h equals b sin A.

69
00:05:13,267 --> 00:05:13,959
Two.

70
00:05:13,959 --> 00:05:18,167
h is fifteen, and twenty lies between fifteen and thirty.

71
00:05:22,533 --> 00:05:27,157
From a point P the top of a tower is at an elevation of thirty degrees.

72
00:05:27,157 --> 00:05:32,574
Walk forty metres towards it, to Q, and the elevation becomes sixty.

73
00:05:32,574 --> 00:05:34,178
How tall is the tower?

74
00:05:34,400 --> 00:05:36,628
Look at triangle P Q S.

75
00:05:36,628 --> 00:05:42,670
The sixty degrees at Q is an exterior angle, so the angle inside at Q is one twenty.

76
00:05:42,670 --> 00:05:44,506
That leaves thirty at S.

77
00:05:44,506 --> 00:05:52,383
Two equal angles: the triangle is isosceles, and Q S equals P Q, forty metres.

78
00:05:52,383 --> 00:06:00,997
In the right triangle at the foot, the height is forty sin sixty: twenty root three, about thirty four point six metres.

79
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:03,293
The surveyor's river.

80
00:06:03,500 --> 00:06:07,648
A baseline and two angles: the third angle is seventy five.

81
00:06:07,867 --> 00:06:10,302
The sine law gives both sloping distances.

82
00:06:10,533 --> 00:06:17,454
The width is the perpendicular from the tree: sixty three point four metres, and the other triangle agrees.

83
00:06:19,467 --> 00:06:20,341
A real field.

84
00:06:20,700 --> 00:06:22,216
s is two seventy.

85
00:06:22,433 --> 00:06:24,967
Heron: nine thousand square metres.

86
00:06:25,167 --> 00:06:28,046
That is seventeen point seven ropani.

87
00:06:28,046 --> 00:06:32,568
Small for such long sides, because the field is long and thin.

88
00:06:35,200 --> 00:06:36,647
Exam tip.

89
00:06:36,647 --> 00:06:38,978
Name the case before you calculate.

90
00:06:38,978 --> 00:06:41,530
Keep five figures until the end.

91
00:06:41,530 --> 00:06:45,771
And remember: half a degree is thirty minutes, not fifty.

92
00:06:47,767 --> 00:06:48,953
Pause and think.

93
00:06:48,953 --> 00:06:50,798
Which data never fixes a triangle?

94
00:06:57,667 --> 00:06:58,945
Three angles.

95
00:06:58,945 --> 00:07:04,649
They fix the shape, but every similar triangle, of any size, has the same angles.

96
00:07:06,833 --> 00:07:07,928
Quick recap.

97
00:07:07,928 --> 00:07:10,259
Three sides: cosine law.

98
00:07:10,259 --> 00:07:14,660
Two sides and the included angle: cosine or tangent law.

99
00:07:14,660 --> 00:07:17,967
One side and two angles: the sine law.

100
00:07:17,967 --> 00:07:25,129
Two sides and a non included angle: compare a with b sin A, and test both possible angles.

101
00:07:25,129 --> 00:07:30,133
And for heights and distances, draw the figure and solve the triangle on the baseline.

102
00:07:34,300 --> 00:07:36,476
That was solution of triangles.

103
00:07:36,476 --> 00:07:41,502
Next, conic sections: the parabola, ellipse and hyperbola.

104
00:07:41,502 --> 00:07:42,085
See you there.

